 ## Overview
-This method accelerates the sphere tracing of isosurfaces defined by Fractal Brownian Motion (FBM) noise by computing valid Sphere Distance Bounds (SDB) and adaptive step sizes using a dynamic Bounding Volume Hierarchy (BVH) derived from the FBM structure itself [S1]. It progressively evaluates octaves while bounding the remaining potential contribution to determine if the total function value can exceed the isosurface threshold, using an Early Stop threshold parameter $k$ to limit octave evaluations [S1]. Unlike naive sphere tracing which evaluates the full FBM at every step, this approach supports features like Adaptive Lipschitz constants, Sub-stepping, Early Stop+k, and Quadratic Taylor inclusion functions [S1].
+This method accelerates the sphere tracing of isosurfaces defined by Fractal Brownian Motion (FBM) noise by computing valid Sphere Distance Bounds (SDB) and adaptive step sizes using a dynamic, implicit Bounding Volume Hierarchy (BVH) derived from the FBM structure itself [S1]. Unlike naive sphere tracing which evaluates the full FBM sum at every step, this approach progressively evaluates octaves while bounding the remaining potential contribution to determine if the total function value can exceed the isosurface threshold, allowing for early termination and larger step sizes [S1]. It builds on [Sphere Tracing and Step Rules](9643-sphere-tracing-and-step-rules.md) by replacing explicit spatial acceleration structures with a procedural hierarchy that reduces the average evaluation cost in empty space from $O(N)$ to $O(k)$, where $k \ll N$ is the number of octaves required to prove emptiness [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The algorithm computes a step size and the final FBM value by iterating through the octaves of the noise function [S1]. It maintains a running sum of the FBM value ($F$), the derivative along the ray ($dF$), and the accumulated step size [S1]. At each octave, it constructs a bounding volume for the remaining octaves to determine if further evaluation is necessary or if a larger step can be taken [S1].
+The algorithm integrates the FBM summation loop with the sphere tracing step computation. Instead of evaluating all $n$ octaves to get a single distance value, it computes the FBM value $F$, the derivative along the ray $dF$, and a valid step size incrementally. It uses a bounding volume $BVH$ representing the maximum possible contribution of the remaining uncomputed octaves to decide whether to compute a step size and whether to terminate the octave loop early.
 
