 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Thread Divergence**: The FBM sum loop may exit earlier for some threads than others due to Early Stop [S1]. However, rays from the same warp are spatially coherent, so divergence is typically limited to a single octave [S1].
-*   **GPU Memory Usage**: The method avoids $O(n)$ per-thread data storage required by dynamic re-ordering methods, making it more suitable for fast GPU implementation [S1].
-*   **Threshold Approximation**: The threshold function $v(\mathbf{p})$ is approximated by its 1st order (affine) Taylor polynomial [S1]. Higher order threshold functions were not implemented as the approximation showed no noticeable error in tested scenes [S1].
-*   **Noise Parameters**: The algorithm requires $\lambda_{noise}$ and $\gamma_{noise}$ for arbitrary noise functions [S1]. Default values depend on the noise type (e.g., Perlin noise with or without absolute value) [S1].
+*   **Thread Divergence**: The Early Stop feature causes thread divergence within a warp, as different rays may exit the octave loop at different iterations. However, rays from the same warp are spatially coherent, so divergence is typically limited to a single octave [S1].
+*   **GPU Memory Usage**: The method avoids $O(n)$ per-thread data storage required by dynamic re-ordering methods [WH96], making it more suitable for fast GPU implementation [S1].
+*   **Threshold Approximation**: The threshold function $v(p)$ is approximated by its 1st order (affine) Taylor polynomial. Higher order approximations were not implemented as they showed no noticeable error improvement [S1].
+*   **Noise Parameters**: The algorithm requires $\lambda_{noise}$ and $\gamma_{noise}$ for arbitrary noise functions. Default values depend on the specific noise implementation (e.g., Perlin with or without abs()) [S1].
 
